{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And he said.</b> All the commentators said with one mouth that the meaning of בי is a language of request. And R. Judah HaLevi said that it is an abbreviated expression, the sense being \"בי אני אדני העון\" (1 Samuel 25:24), that is, impose upon me whatever punishment you wish and release me from this, that you not send me. And similarly \"בי אדני ידבר נא עבדך דבר\" (Genesis 44:18) — do to me whatever you wish and let me speak." "<b>A man of words.</b> One who knows how to speak eloquently, who is not hindered in his tongue when speaking or stammering, or whom certain letters sit heavily upon his mouth. And the practice of the Hebrews, when they wish to coordinate two or three things, is to add the word גם at the beginning, as in \"משל בנו גם אתה, גם בנך, גם בן בנך\" (Judges 5:22)." "And the meaning of שלשם: it does not mean yesterday, but rather up to this day on which he is speaking — and behold, a third day has passed up to this day on which he is speaking. And the mem is according to the manner of plural language, on account of the day and its yesterday. And even though the shin is not qamatz-gadol as is the rule, or the mem is added at the end as it is added at the beginning: \"ויהי כמשלש חדשים\" (Genesis 35:24)." "<b>Also from when You spoke.</b> From the time You spoke, as in \"ומאז באתי אל פרעה לדבר בשמך\" (below, 5:23), for the heaviness of his tongue did not depart but remained as it was. And one who says he forgot the Egyptian language is not correct, for he says two things: כבד פה and כבד לשון, and furthermore we learn from HaShem's response: \"Who placed a mouth for man, or who makes mute\" — that He is not speaking about the Egyptian language, but rather thus was he born, that he was heavy of mouth so that he could not produce the letters of the lip and all the letters of the tongue, but only some of them he would produce with difficulty. And this is the meaning of \"I will be with your mouth and I will teach you\": He said that He would show him which words to speak, in which there are none of the letters that sit heavily upon his mouth."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes Judah HaLevi's interpretation; the phrase 'אני אדני העון' is Abigail's words to David, here read as 'I am my lord's guilt' = 'impose the punishment on me.'", 2 "Ibn Ezra cites Genesis 35:24 but the phrase ויהי כמשלש חדשים appears at Genesis 38:24; the chapter citation may reflect a variant numbering or scribal error in the source."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:31:38.819580482Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}